Body:
@rekram1-node 

`Started` and `Ended` are sent almost together, any idea why the session.wait doesn't hold back the `Ended`? Thanks!

```javascript
import { createOpencodeClient } from "@opencode-ai/sdk/v2/client";

const pending = new Map();

async function waitForCompletion(client, sessionID, promptText) {
  try {
    client.v2.session.wait({ sessionID });
    console.log(`Ended: ${promptText}`);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("[session-plugin] wait error:", err);
  } finally {
    pending.delete(sessionID);
  }
}

export default async function sessionPlugin(input) {
  const v2Client = createOpencodeClient({
    baseUrl: input.serverUrl.toString(),
  });

  return {
    "chat.message": async (msgInput, output) => {
      const { sessionID } = msgInput;
      const parts = output.parts ?? [];
      const text = parts.map((p) => (p.type === "text" ? p.text : "")).filter(Boolean).join("\n");
      if (!text) return;

      const prev = pending.get(sessionID);
      if (prev) {
        pending.set(sessionID, `${prev}\n${text}`);
        return;
      }

      console.log(`Started: ${text}`);

      pending.set(sessionID, text);
      waitForCompletion(v2Client, sessionID, text);
    },
  };
}
```

This is a real risk pattern for credit-based billing.

One thing I would add to the proposed fix: guarding only by Stripe `event.id` is useful, but the safer pattern is also to guard the underlying payment object used for the balance mutation, for example `payment_id`, invoice ID, checkout session ID, or payment intent ID.

A minimal acceptance test would be:

1. first delivery increments balance;
2. same Stripe event ID does not increment again;
3. different Stripe event ID for the same payment object also does not increment again;
4. DB unique constraint prevents duplicate `PaymentTable.payment_id`.

I built a small local proof asset for this exact pattern here:
https://github.com/Mutaner/webhook-rescue-kit

It may be useful as a reference test shape, not as a production dependency.
